    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d114">

                <pb facs="0123" n="117" sameAs="frus:frus1894app1/pg117"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr.
                            Gresham.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Embassy of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1893-09-19"><hi rendition="#italic">September 19,
                        1893</hi></date>. (Received September 30.)</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: * * * I have to-day received a note from
                    the foreign office informing me that a letter has just been received at the
                    colonial office from Sir Charles Tupper, in Canada, stating that he and the
                    prime <pb facs="0124" n="118" xml:id="pg118"/> minister hard been engrossed by
                    arrears of business since their return, but that he was preparing a memorandum
                    on the award in the Bering Sea Arbitration; and I am further informed that Lord
                    Ripon has already telegraphed to Canada to expedite the memorandum in question
                    and its receipt at the colonial office here.</p>
                <p>I have every confidence that an effective execution of the award will be agreed
                    upon in as short a time as the complexity and magnitude of the subject, and the
                    somewhat undefined nature of the regulations and recommendations of the Tribunal
                    of Arbitration, will admit.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
